        {
          "line": 87,
          "text": "1.1 The geometry and the cohomology of the projective line. The fiber products are $Z_{12}=Z_{21}=\\mathbb P^1$. By [F3] its Schubert CW structure has one $0$-cell and one $2$-cell, so the cellular cochain complex is $k\\oplus k[-2]$. By [F4] it computes singular cohomology, and [F6] identifies this with constant-sheaf cohomology. Choosing cocycles representing the two generators gives a quasi-isomorphism $k\\oplus k[-2]\\to R\\Gamma(\\mathbb P^1,k)$. Hence $L_2\\simeq k[1]\\oplus k[-1]$ and $L_1=k$. [F3, F4, F6, algebra]"
        },
        {
          "line": 88,
          "text": ""
        },
        {
          "line": 89,
          "text": "2.1 The two classes and their composites. The unit $\\alpha:k\\to R\\Gamma(\\mathbb P^1,k)$ includes the degree-zero summand, generating $\\operatorname{Ext}^{-1}(L_1,L_2)=k$. Evaluation at a point projects to that summand; its shift $\\beta:L_2\\to k[1]$ generates $\\operatorname{Ext}^1(L_2,L_1)=k$, since $\\operatorname{Hom}(k[-2],k)=0$. Therefore $\\beta[-1]\\circ\\alpha=\\operatorname{id}_k$. The reverse composite $\\alpha[1]\\circ\\beta$ is also nonzero: it is the projector of $L_2=k[1]\\oplus k[-1]$ onto $k[1]$, and is neither zero nor $\\operatorname{id}_{L_2}$. [step 1.1, algebra]"
        },
        {
          "line": 90,
          "text": ""
        },
        {
          "line": 91,
          "text": "3.1 The convolution check. Apply [F2] with $(i,j,k)=(1,2,1)$ and degrees $r=-1$, $s=1$ to the composable pair $\\alpha:L_1\\to L_2[r]$ and $\\beta:L_2\\to L_1[s]$: since $\\beta[r]\\circ\\alpha=\\beta[-1]\\circ\\alpha=\\operatorname{id}_k$ by step 2.1, the product theorem gives $\\Phi_{11}^{0}(\\operatorname{id}_k)=\\Phi_{12}^{-1}(\\alpha)*\\Phi_{21}^{1}(\\beta)$, so $\\alpha*\\beta=\\operatorname{id}_k$ in the source-first convention, and the right-hand side is the convolution product of [F1]. The corner degrees are $d_1+d_2-r=2$ for $\\alpha$ and $d_2+d_1-s=0$ for $\\beta$. [F1, F2, step 1.1, step 2.1]"
        },
