          "text": "4.1 The geometric product. The adjunction chain of [F1] sends $\\alpha$ to $\\operatorname{id}_{k_{\\mathbb P^1}}$, hence to the positive fundamental class $[\\mathbb P^1]$. Write $i:\\mathrm{pt}\\hookrightarrow\\mathbb P^1$ for the evaluation point. Evaluation is the global-sections image of $k_{\\mathbb P^1}\\to i_*k$, and its adjoint under $R\\Gamma\\dashv a^!$, $a:\\mathbb P^1\\to\\mathrm{pt}$, is the composite $k_{\\mathbb P^1}\\to i_*k=i_*i^!\\omega_{\\mathbb P^1}\\to\\omega_{\\mathbb P^1}$, with the last arrow the proper trace. Thus $\\Phi_{21}^{1}(\\beta)=i_*[\\mathrm{pt}]$ with coefficient $+1$. Geometrically $\\mathbb P^1\\times\\{i\\}$ meets the middle diagonal of $\\mathbb P^1\\times\\mathbb P^1$ transversely at $(i,i)$; the map from its tangent line to the complex normal line of the diagonal is an isomorphism, preserving the real orientation. The positive Thom cap of [F5] therefore gives that point with multiplicity $+1$, and proper pushforward to $Z_{11}=\\mathrm{pt}$ gives $[\\mathrm{pt}]$. This computes $[\\mathbb P^1]*[\\mathrm{pt}]=[\\mathrm{pt}]$, with middle degree drop $2$ and no permutation sign, agreeing with step 3.1. It verifies a transverse instance of the calculus, not its general nontransverse case. AC is inherited through [A1]. [A1, F1, F2, F5, step 2.1, step 3.1] \u220e"
